<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-photosensors">
    <title>Photosensors and the Solar Tracker</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to build a solar tracker: a servomotor that
            turns an arm carrying two light sensors until the arm points at a light
            source, and that keeps following the source as it moves.  The sensor is a
            photocell, a resistor whose resistance falls as more light falls on it.
            The ADC (analog-to-digital converter) measures voltages and not
            resistances, so the first job is an interface: a voltage divider that
            turns the photocell's resistance into a voltage on an ADC pin.  With two
            photocells on the servo's arm, one on each side, the difference between
            their two voltages depends on which side the light is on, and a program we
            write turns that difference into a change in the servo's command pulse.
            That program is a feedback loop.  The servo has its own feedback loop
            inside it, and the tracker closes a second loop around the servo.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day.  Before class, the reading introduces
            the photocell, the family of sensors that work the same way, the divider,
            the photocell's datasheet, and what a solar tracker is.  In class we'll
            predict which way the divider's voltage moves as the light changes, work
            through the photocell study from Lab 8, mount the photocells on the arm,
            discuss how the tracker's feedback loop can be implemented, and start on
            the loop.  Lab 8 is where you tune and demonstrate it.
            <xref ref="sec-photo-reference"/> collects the divider formula, the
            two-channel ADC read and the loop's update rule for looking up later.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain what a CdS photocell is: a resistor whose resistance falls as more light falls on it, inexpensive, nonlinear, and so variable from unit to unit that it is used to compare light levels rather than to measure them.</li>
                <li>Put a resistive sensor into a voltage divider, state the divider's formula, and predict which way its output moves as the sensor's resistance changes.</li>
                <li>Read the photocell's datasheet for what the tracker needs: the sensitivity as the slope of a log-log plot, the rise and fall times as a bound on how fast to sample, and the test conditions behind every number.</li>
                <li>Recognize the same divider interface across a family of resistance-based sensors, and identify which sensors need more than a divider.</li>
                <li>Mount two photocells on the servo's arm, wire both dividers to two ADC channels, and print both readings.</li>
                <li>Design the tracker's control loop from Lab 8's description: the error, the update rule, its sign, its bounds, the sampling interval, and what a gain of 0.01 means in integer arithmetic.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-photo-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: The Photocell and the Voltage Divider</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the ADC chapter we measured a voltage from a potentiometer, and in the
                servo chapter we turned that voltage into a position.  The solar tracker
                replaces the potentiometer with a sensor that responds to light.  This
                reading covers what that sensor is, the family of sensors that work the
                same way, how a resistance becomes a voltage the ADC can measure, what the
                sensor's datasheet says, and what a solar tracker is.  In class we'll
                build the circuit and discuss the loop.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-cell">
            <title>What a Photocell Is</title>
            <p>
                A <term>photocell</term> is a resistor whose resistance depends on how
                much light falls on its face (<xref ref="fig-photocell-photo"/>).  It is
                also called a CdS cell, after the cadmium sulfide it is made of, a
                <term>light-dependent resistor</term> (LDR), or a photoresistor.
                Cadmium sulfide's resistivity decreases with increasing light exposure.
                A photocell is made of semiconductor material with a high resistance,
                because very few of its electrons are free to move: the vast majority are
                locked into the crystal lattice and cannot move.  As light falls on the
                semiconductor, photons are absorbed by the lattice and some of their
                energy is transferred to the electrons.  The amount of energy transferred
                gives some of the electrons sufficient energy to break free from the
                crystal lattice, so that they can then conduct electricity, and the
                resistance of the photocell falls.  The process is progressive: as more
                light shines on the photocell, more electrons are released, and the
                resistance falls further.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-photo">
                <caption>A CdS photocell.  The face is the cadmium sulfide: the dark
                meander is the sensing material, and the two posts are where the leads
                attach.  The two leads are the resistor's two terminals, and there is
                no polarity.  Image from
                <url href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/161">Adafruit</url>.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide03_img1.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Photocells are small, inexpensive, low-power and easy to use, and for
                that reason they often appear in toys, gadgets and appliances.  They do
                age, and a cell's resistance drifts over time.  They are also very
                inaccurate.  Each
                photocell will act a little differently from the next, even if the two
                are from the same batch, and the variation can be really large, 50% or
                higher, so a photocell should not be used to determine a precise light
                level in <term>lux</term> (lumens per square meter, the unit of
                illuminance).  Instead, you can expect to be able to determine basic
                light changes, and we will not use a photocell to measure how bright the
                light is.  The
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/photocells">Adafruit guide to
                photocells</url> covers the same ground and is worth reading.  Its
                wiring examples use a different board; our circuit is in
                <xref ref="subsec-photo-divider"/>.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-family">
            <title>Sensors Based on Resistance</title>
            <p>
                Many sensors are based on a variable resistance:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>A potentiometer, linear or rotary, senses position or angle: its resistance from the wiper to either end depends on where the wiper is, which is how it gave us a voltage in the ADC chapter.</li>
                <li>A force-sensing resistor changes its resistance with the force pressing on it.</li>
                <li>A photoresistor changes its resistance with light.</li>
                <li>A thermistor changes its resistance with temperature.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The photoresistor, the thermistor and the force-sensing resistor are read
                with the same circuit, the voltage divider of the next subsection, and
                one ADC channel each; the potentiometer needs no fixed resistor, because
                its own track contains both halves of the divider.  Two more sensors are also
                based on resistance but need more than a divider:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>A strain gage senses a tiny stretch or compression as a very small change in resistance.</li>
                <li>A load cell measures weight, and is built from strain gages.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Those two require more complex electronics than a simple voltage
                divider, because the change to be measured is so small.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-divider">
            <title>The Voltage Divider as the Interface</title>
            <p>
                The ADC measures a voltage, and a photocell is a resistance.  The circuit
                that turns one into the other is a <term>voltage divider</term>: the
                sensor is placed in series with a fixed resistor between the supply and
                ground, with the node between them going to an ADC pin
                (<xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>).  Call the sensor's resistance
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> and the fixed resistor <m>R_M</m>.  The voltage at
                the node is
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot \frac{R_M}{R_\text{sens} + R_M}</mrow>
                </md>
                which resembles the potentiometer's formula from the ADC chapter
                (<xref ref="fig-adc-divider"/>), with <m>R_\text{sens}</m> in place of
                <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_M</m> in place of <m>R_2</m>.  <m>V_{cc}</m> is the
                Nucleo's 3.3 V, because the ADC's reference is 3.3 V
                (<xref ref="subsec-adc-quantization"/>): a node fed from 3.3 V can use
                the ADC's whole range and never exceeds it, and a voltage above the
                ADC's reference is outside the pin's operating conditions.  As the light
                changes, <m>R_\text{sens}</m> changes and <m>V_M</m> changes with it.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-divider">
                <caption>The voltage divider as the interface between a resistive
                sensor and the ADC.  The sensor, <m>R_\text{sens}</m>, drawn as a
                variable resistor, is connected from <m>V_{cc}</m> to the node, and
                the fixed resistor <m>R_M</m> from the node to ground.  The node's
                voltage <m>V_M</m> goes to an ADC pin, and the formula gives it in
                terms of the two resistances.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/fig-photocell-divider.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-datasheet">
            <title>The Photocell's Datasheet</title>
            <p>
                Our photocell is the PDV-P8001, and its
                <url href="external/datasheets/CdS-photocell-PDV-P8001.pdf">datasheet</url>
                is one page long, so look through the whole of it.  The description says that
                these light dependent resistors are available in a wide range of
                resistance values and are packaged in a two-leaded plastic-coated
                ceramic header; the package drawing gives the dimensions, and there is no
                polarity.  The <em>Absolute maximum rating</em> table gives
                the largest voltage that may be applied across the cell (150 V), the
                largest power it may dissipate, and the temperature range; our 3.3 V
                circuit comes nowhere near any of them.  The <em>Electro-optical
                characteristics</em> table is the one the tracker depends on, and every
                row carries its test conditions, at 23 °C throughout.  The illuminated
                resistance is measured under 10 lux from a lamp at 2856 K (a tungsten
                lamp); the dark resistance is measured 10 seconds after that light is
                removed, because the resistance keeps rising for a while after the
                light goes; the sensitivity is defined between 100 lux and 10 lux from
                the same lamp; and the rise and fall times are for a step to and from
                10 lux.  A row often lists only a range, a minimum and a maximum, and a
                number from a datasheet means what it says only under its conditions.
                The rows are the dark resistance and the illuminated resistance, which
                in Lab 8 you are asked to look up; the sensitivity, which we'll look at in
                class on a plot; the spectral range, 400 to 700 nm, with the peak
                response at 520 nm, in the green, near the middle of the range the eye
                sees; and the rise time and fall time, 55 ms and 20 ms, which say how
                fast the resistance responds when the light changes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The light levels in the table are in lux, which is not a unit most of
                us have a feel for.  The
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/photocells">Adafruit guide</url>
                has a table of examples, and a few of its rows give the scale:
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Illuminance</cell>
                    <cell>Example</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>0.27 to 1 lux</cell><cell>full moon on a clear night</cell></row>
                <row><cell>50 lux</cell><cell>a family living room</cell></row>
                <row><cell>300 to 500 lux</cell><cell>a well-lit office</cell></row>
                <row><cell>1,000 lux</cell><cell>an overcast day</cell></row>
                <row><cell>10,000 to 25,000 lux</cell><cell>full daylight, not direct sun</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-photo-tracker">
            <title>What a Solar Tracker Is</title>
            <p>
                A solar tracker uses servomotors to keep a solar panel pointed at the sun
                throughout the day.  Some trackers work by attaching photosensors at the
                edges of the solar panel, and using the imbalance between the sensors
                when the light arrives from one side to control servos in elevation
                (tilting up and down) as well as in azimuth (turning side to side).  In
                Lab 8 we build a model of an azimuth-only system, one that only turns
                side to side: the servo from the servo chapter, with the arm bolted to
                its horn (<xref ref="fig-servo-leads"/>), carries a photocell at each
                end, and each photocell is set into a shielding cup that shades it from
                light arriving from the other side.  When the arm points at the light,
                the two photocells receive the same illumination; when it does not, the
                photocell on the side nearer the light receives more.  A feedback loop
                in a program adjusts the servo's position until both sensors receive
                the same illumination.  Writing that program is Lab 8's design
                challenge.  This is not how most solar trackers work in the real world:
                an installed tracker usually computes where the sun is from the date,
                the time and its own location, and points the panel by calculation,
                with no light sensor at all.  Our sensor-driven model is a way to build
                and understand a feedback loop, not a copy of the installation.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-photo">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-resistance">
                <statement>
                    <p>A photocell is in a lit room, and a hand is placed over it.  What
                    happens to its resistance?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It rises, and it stays high for as long as the cell is covered.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  With less light, fewer electrons are freed
                        from the lattice, so fewer are available to conduct and the
                        resistance rises.  It stays high until light returns.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It falls, because the cell is no longer being driven by light.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Light is what frees the electrons that conduct.  Less
                        light means fewer free electrons, which is a higher resistance,
                        not a lower one.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It rises briefly and then returns to its lit value.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The resistance depends on the light falling on the
                        cell at the time.  It does not return to a previous value while
                        the cell stays covered.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It does not change; resistance is a fixed property of the part.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>For a plain resistor that is true.  A photocell is a
                        resistor whose resistance depends on the light on its face, which
                        is what makes it a sensor.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-variation">
                <statement>
                    <p>Two photocells of the same part number, from the same bag, are
                    placed side by side under the same lamp, and their resistances differ
                    by a third.  What is the most likely explanation?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Nothing is wrong: photocells vary from one unit to the next by that much, and the datasheet gives ranges rather than one value.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The Adafruit guide puts the variation at
                        50% or higher, even within a batch, and the datasheet says the
                        part is available in a wide range of resistance values.  A
                        tracker built from two such cells has to allow for the
                        difference between them.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One of the two cells is defective.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A difference of a third is within the normal variation
                        for these parts.  A defective cell would read open, or would not
                        change with light at all.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The cells need a few minutes under the lamp before they agree.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The rise and fall times on the datasheet are tens of
                        milliseconds, so both cells respond in tens of milliseconds, not
                        minutes.  The difference is between the cells themselves.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The multimeter is inaccurate at these resistances.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A multimeter reads resistances in the kilohm range to
                        within a percent or so.  The difference is in the parts.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-swapped">
                <statement>
                    <p>Suppose the divider is wired the other way around from
                    <xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>: the fixed resistor from
                    <m>V_{cc}</m> to the node, and the photocell from the node to ground.
                    When the light on the photocell gets brighter, what happens to
                    <m>V_M</m>?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It falls.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  With the photocell at the bottom, the
                        formula becomes <m>V_M = V_{cc} \cdot R_\text{sens}/(R_M +
                        R_\text{sens})</m>: the photocell's resistance is now in the
                        numerator, and as it falls the fraction falls.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It rises.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Follow the formula with the two resistors exchanged,
                        with round numbers: with <m>R_M</m> at 10 kΩ, a cell at 20 kΩ
                        gives <m>3.3 \times 20/30 = 2.2</m> V, and when the light
                        brightens it to 10 kΩ, <m>3.3 \times 10/20 = 1.65</m> V.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It does not change, because the same two resistors are in series either way.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The current through the two is the same either way,
                        but <m>V_M</m> is the voltage across whichever resistor is at the
                        bottom, and that has changed.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-peak">
                <statement>
                    <p>From the PDV-P8001 datasheet, at what wavelength is the photocell's
                    response strongest?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>520 nm, which is green light.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The spectral application range is 400 to
                        700 nm and the peak is at 520 nm, so the cell responds to the
                        colors the eye sees best.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>400 nm, at the violet end of its range.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>400 nm is the lower end of the spectral range.  The peak
                        is a separate row.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>700 nm, at the red end of its range.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>700 nm is the upper end of the spectral range.  The peak
                        is a separate row.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>2856 nm, in the infrared.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>2856 K is a temperature, not a wavelength: it is the
                        color temperature of the lamp used for the table's test
                        conditions.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-photo-family">
                <statement>
                    <p>Which of these sensors needs more than a voltage divider and an ADC
                    channel to be read?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>A strain gage.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A strain gage's resistance changes by a very
                        small fraction of its value, too small a change for a divider and
                        our ADC to resolve, so strain gages and the load cells built from
                        them require more complex electronics.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A thermistor.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A thermistor's resistance changes a great deal with
                        temperature, and a divider reads it directly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A force-sensing resistor.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A force-sensing resistor changes its resistance by a
                        large factor with force, and a divider reads it directly.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A potentiometer.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A potentiometer is its own divider, which is how it gave
                        the ADC a voltage in the ADC chapter.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-photo-day16">
        <title>Day 16 In-Class: Photosensors and the Solar Tracker</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll put the photocell from the reading into a voltage divider and
                predict which way its voltage moves as the light changes.  Then we'll
                work through the photocell study from Lab 8, add the photocells to the
                tracker arm of your servo, and discuss how the feedback loop can be
                implemented.  The rest of the class is time to start on the loop, which
                is your lab work.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 10 min = 4 (her slide 7 commit and its reveal) + 4 (her slides 4-5, the nonlinearity) + 2 (compare two) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-divider">
            <title>Part 1: The Photocell in the Divider</title>
            <p>
                The photocell is the sensor <m>R_\text{sens}</m> in the divider of
                <xref ref="fig-photocell-divider"/>, with the fixed resistor
                <m>R_M</m> and the node voltage <m>V_M</m>.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-divider-recall" ref="fig-photocell-divider">
    <caption>The photocell is the sensor <m>R_\text{sens}</m> in the divider, with the fixed resistor <m>R_M</m> and the node voltage <m>V_M</m>.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The reading's figure (refPage), image-dominant so the subscripts read from the back; then her slide 7's question as the activity.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-direction">
                <title>As the light gets brighter</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Discuss at your table: as the light gets brighter, does
                    <m>V_M</m> increase or decrease?  Explain your answer from the
                    divider's formula.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> is in the denominator.  As the light gets brighter
                <m>R_\text{sens}</m> falls, the denominator <m>R_\text{sens} + R_M</m>
                falls, and <m>V_M</m> rises.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-direction-reveal" instructor="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>R_sens is in the denominator. As the light gets brighter R_sens falls, the denominator R_sens + R_M falls, and V_M rises.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>REVEAL, after the activity. ≈ 1 min.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                The CdS photocell has a nonlinear response: its resistance is not a
                linear function of the illumination
                (<xref ref="fig-photocell-loglog"/>, taken from the
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/photocells">Adafruit guide</url>).
                The figure is a log-log plot.
                Both axes are logarithmic, so each labeled step along either axis, 1k
                to 10k or 10 lux to 100 lux, is a factor of ten.  On these axes a
                relationship of the form <m>R \propto E^{-S}</m> is a straight line,
                where <m>R</m> is the resistance, <m>E</m> is the illumination and
                <m>S</m> is a constant.  The nonlinearity is not a problem if the
                photocell is used as a light-or-dark sensor, for a night light for
                instance, or for our tracker.  If we wanted a quantitative result in
                lux, we would have to implement a conversion function.  The PDV-P8001
                datasheet's <em>Sensitivity</em> row gives <m>S = 0.6</m> for our
                part, so its approximate relationship is
                <md number="no">
                    <mrow>\log_{10}(R_{100}) - \log_{10}(R_{10}) = -0.6\,[\log_{10}(E_{100}) - \log_{10}(E_{10})]</mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>R_{100}</m> and <m>R_{10}</m> are the cell's resistances at
                100 lux and at 10 lux, and <m>E_{100}</m> and <m>E_{10}</m> are those
                two illuminances.  In words: when the illumination increases by a
                factor of 10, the resistance decreases by a factor of <m>10^{0.6}</m>,
                which is about 4.  The curves in <xref ref="fig-photocell-loglog"/> are
                the Adafruit guide's generic family, and they are steeper than that.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-photocell-loglog">
                <caption>Resistance against illumination for a family of CdS photocells,
                from the Adafruit guide, with one curve highlighted in red.  Both axes are
                logarithmic, and each curve is close to a straight line on these axes:
                resistance falls by roughly a constant factor for every factor of ten in
                illumination.  This is the guide's generic family, not the PDV-P8001's
                own curve.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide04_0c98f286.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-nonlinear" ref="fig-photocell-loglog">
    <ul>
        <li>The CdS photocell has a nonlinear response: its resistance is not a linear function of the illumination.</li>
        <li>The figure, taken from the Adafruit guide, is a log-log plot. Both axes are logarithmic, so each labeled step along either axis, 1k to 10k or 10 lux to 100 lux, is a factor of ten.</li>
        <li>The nonlinearity is not a problem if the photocell is used as a light-or-dark sensor, for a night light for instance, or for our tracker. If we wanted a quantitative result in lux, we would have to implement a conversion function.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Her slide 4.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-loglog-relation" ref="fig-photocell-loglog">
    <p>The PDV-P8001 datasheet's Sensitivity row gives S = 0.6 for our part, so the approximate relationship is:</p>
    <p>log10(R100) − log10(R10) = −0.6 [log10(E100) − log10(E10)]</p>
    <p>R100 and R10 are the cell's resistances at 100 lux and at 10 lux; E100 and E10 are those two illuminances; lux = lumens per square meter.</p>
    <p>In words: when the illumination increases by a factor of 10, the resistance decreases by a factor of 10^0.6, which is about 4. A relationship of this form, R = C·E^(−S), plots as a straight line on log-log axes.</p>
    <caption>The plot is the Adafruit guide's generic family, not the PDV-P8001's own curve; its curves are steeper.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. Her slide 5's shape: the equation on its own line in her plain-text form, the symbols as a legend line.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                Each photocell is a bit different, even if two are <q>the same</q> part,
                which is why the datasheet gives ranges rather than one number.  The
                tracker compares the two cells: when the arm points at the light, the
                two cells receive the same illumination, and if they had the same
                resistance-versus-illumination curve they would give the same voltage.
                Two real cells do not have the same curve.  Under the same light they
                give different voltages, so the two voltages are equal at some arm
                position that is not quite pointed at the light.  How to deal with that
                difference between your two cells is part of your work in Lab 8.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-compare">
    <ul>
        <li>Each photocell is a bit different, even if two are "the same" part, which is why the datasheet gives ranges rather than one number.</li>
        <li>The tracker compares the two cells: when the arm points at the light, the two cells receive the same illumination, and if they had the same resistance-versus-illumination curve they would give the same voltage.</li>
        <li>Two real cells do not have the same curve. Under the same light they give different voltages, so the two voltages are equal at some arm position that is not quite pointed at the light. How to deal with that difference between your two cells is part of your work in Lab 8.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 38 min = 1 (lab time, the pot comes off) + 37 (Part 1 of Lab 8, at
             the table; checkpoints about minute 39 and 53) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-lab">
            <title>Part 2: Part 1 of Lab 8, Technical Study of Photocells</title>
            <p>
                We'll now give you some time to work through Part 1 of Lab 8, where you
                will go deeper into the datasheet.  First, with nothing powered, take the
                potentiometer off the breadboard: its channel, A0 (PA0), is where the
                first photocell's divider goes.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-lab-time">
    <ul>
        <li>We'll now give you some time to work through Part 1 of Lab 8, where you will go deeper into the datasheet.</li>
        <li>First, with nothing powered, take the potentiometer off the breadboard: its channel, A0 (PA0), is where the first photocell's divider goes.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then Lab 8's Figure 1 and the deliverables, to leave up while they work, ≈ 37 min in all; checkpoints about minute 39 and 53.</note>
</slide>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-lab-fig1">
                <caption>Lab 8's Figure 1, the photocell voltage divider: <m>R_1</m>
                is the photocell, <m>R_2</m> the fixed resistor, and <m>V_1</m> the
                node.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/lab8-fig1-divider.png" width="35%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-lab-fig1" ref="fig-day16-lab-fig1">
    <caption>Lab 8's Figure 1: R1 is the photocell, R2 the fixed resistor, and V1 the node.</caption>
    <note>Her slide 8's figure. The deliverables follow as the activity.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-study">
                <title>Part 1 of Lab 8: Technical Study of Photocells</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d1">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 1.  Using the
                    <url href="external/datasheets/CdS-photocell-PDV-P8001.pdf">PDV-P8001 datasheet</url>,
                    record the minimum dark resistance and minimum illuminated
                    resistance for <m>R_1</m>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d2">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 2.  Using your multimeter and a flashlight,
                    record the dark resistance and illuminated resistance for
                    <m>R_1</m>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d3">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 3.  Why might the measured values disagree
                    with the datasheet?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d4">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 4.  It is recommended that the value of
                    <m>R_2</m> be set as the geometric mean of <m>R_\text{dark}</m> and
                    <m>R_\text{illuminated}</m>, i.e.
                    <m>R_2 = \sqrt{R_\text{dark} \times R_\text{illuminated}}</m>.  Based
                    on the values you measured, compute <m>R_2</m>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d5">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 5.  Recall that the equation for the voltage
                    divider in Lab 8's Figure 1 is
                    <m>V_1/V_{cc} = R_2/(R_1 + R_2)</m>.  Using the values you measured,
                    roughly what are the voltages that you would expect to see at
                    <m>V_1</m> when the sensor is illuminated and when the sensor is
                    dark, assuming <m>V_{cc} = 3.3</m> V?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d6">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 6.  You are limited by the discrete resistor
                    values made available for your use in the laboratory, so you might
                    not have the exact value you calculated.  Assume that you have a
                    10 kΩ resistor available to you.  Repeat the calculations from the
                    previous deliverable.  Is using a 10 kΩ going to be a
                    problem?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-lab-fig2">
                <caption>Lab 8's Figure 2, the two photocell dividers on the breadboard.
                The Nucleo's 3.3 V and GND feed the two rails.  Each divider is a
                photocell from the 3.3 V rail to a node row and a 10 kΩ resistor from
                that row to the ground rail; one node goes to A0 (PA0) and the other to
                A1 (PA1).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide09_a9c74b15.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-lab-fig2" ref="fig-day16-lab-fig2">
    <caption>Each divider is a photocell from the 3.3 V rail to a node row and a 10 kΩ resistor from that row to the ground rail. One node goes to A0 (PA0) and the other to A1 (PA1).</caption>
    <note>Her slide 9's figure. Then the continued activity: the program, Deliverables 7 and 8.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-two-channel">
                <title>Technical Study of Photocells, continued</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Wire the two photosensors and 10 kΩ resistors on your breadboard as
                    shown in Lab 8's Figure 2.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-program">
                    <statement><p>Write a program to measure the two sensor voltages and
                    print them on your screen.  This should be an easy two-channel
                    modification of the earlier A/D + potentiometer program.  A
                    one-second sampling period is fine.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d7">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 7.  Also wire the sensor voltages to analog
                    channels 1 and 2 of the AD2, to display the voltages with Waveforms'
                    voltmeter function.  Do your measured values for each channel fall
                    within the expected range you calculated earlier?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-d8">
                    <statement><p>Deliverable 8.  Slowly sweep a flashlight beam (the one on
                    your phone is fine) across the two sensors and observe how the
                    voltages change.  Note the maximum and minimum voltages.  How do they
                    compare to your theoretical maximum and minimum voltages?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-two-channel">
                <p>The two-channel loop, with Lab 5's names (<c>adc_setChannel()</c>,
                <c>adc_getValue()</c>).  The delay is fine for the study; the tracker's
                loop replaces it with <c>milliseconds()</c>.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    while (1) {
        adc_setChannel(0);
        v0 = adc_getValue();          // the divider on A0 (PA0)
        adc_setChannel(1);
        v1 = adc_getValue();          // the divider on A1 (PA1)
        printf("A0=%d  A1=%d\n\r", v0, v1);
        delay_ms(1000);
    }
]]></code></program>
                <p>A library whose <c>adc_setChannel()</c> writes <c>CHSELR</c> without
                waiting for <c>CCRDY</c> has its <c>ADSTART</c> write ignored (RM0490,
                <c>ADC_CR</c>), so no conversion starts: the read that follows either
                blocks on <c>EOC</c> or returns the previous conversion's result, which
                is the old channel's, one read late.  A student whose two values look
                swapped, or lag the light by one sample, has that; the fix is the wait,
                in the Reference section.  PA1 needs no <c>MODER</c> change:
                <c>GPIOA_MODER</c> resets to 0xEBFFFFFF (RM0490 §6.4.1), so every
                analog-capable pin starts in analog mode; Day 7 set PA0 explicitly, so
                this will be asked.  Deliverable 1's rows, for checking: dark resistance
                0.2 MΩ minimum after 10 s at 10 lux; illuminated resistance 3 kΩ to
                11 kΩ at 10 lux.</p>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-part2-checkpoint">
                <p>Two checkpoints while they work, about minute 39 (the dividers wired
                and on the voltmeter) and minute 53 (the program printing).  Minute 53
                is a hard stop: a student who is not printing both channels finishes
                Deliverables 7 and 8 as the first step of Lab 8 rather than holding the
                room.  A student whose servo is not working goes on with the study anyway:
                nothing in this Part needs the servo, and the arm's electrical work in
                Part 3 does not either.  A student whose arm already carries its photocells in the cups brings
                their leads to the multimeter and the breadboard through the alligator
                clips.  The dark measurement for Deliverable 2 is taken with the cell
                covered.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Minute 39.  A node at 3.3 V or 0 V: the photocell and its resistor
                    are in different rows, or the jumper from the rail is in the wrong
                    row.  A node that does not change when the cell is covered: the cell's
                    leads are not both in the breadboard.  Two nodes that read the same:
                    the two dividers share a row.</li>
                    <li>Minute 53.  Two channels that print the same value while the
                    voltmeter reads two different nodes: the channel is not being
                    changed between reads, or the second call selects the same channel.
                    Values that are swapped relative to the AD2: the wires to A0 and A1
                    are exchanged, which does not matter for the tracker, or the missing
                    CCRDY wait above.  A value that drifts, or makes no sense against a
                    sensible voltmeter reading, with no matching fault on the breadboard:
                    the program is reading a channel other than the one on the
                    node.</li>
                </ul>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 17 min = 1 review + 1 cup + 1 clips + 11 assembly + 3 checkpoint, about minute 70 -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-arm">
            <title>Part 3: Solar Tracker Assembly</title>
            <p>
                Recall the servo test setup from Day 15: the potentiometer, the ADC and
                the PWM pulse controlled the servo, with the code from
                <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c> that you completed, and the servo powered
                as in <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/>.  The potentiometer came off the
                breadboard when you started Part 1 of Lab 8.  Now we'll add the
                photocells to the tracker arm of your servo.  The arm has a shielding cup at each end
                (<xref ref="fig-day16-cup"/>).
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-servo-review" ref="fig-servo-powering">
    <caption>On Day 15 the potentiometer, the ADC and the PWM pulse controlled the servo, with the code from Day15_servo_template.c that you completed. The powering has not changed: red to the regulator board's 5V row, brown to the ground rail, orange to D11 (PA7). The drawing does not show the potentiometer, which came off the breadboard at the start of Part 1 of Lab 8. Now we'll add the photocells to the tracker arm of your servo.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 10; Day 15's powering drawing (refPage), image-dominant as on Day 15.</note>
</slide>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-cup">
                <caption>A photocell in the shielding cup at one end of the tracker arm.
                The cup shades the cell from light arriving from the other side of the
                arm.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide11_324c26cf.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-cup" ref="fig-day16-cup">
    <caption>Remove your photocells from the breadboard and insert them into the shielding cups on the motor arm, like so. The cup shades its cell from light arriving from the other side of the arm.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 11.</note>
</slide>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-clips">
                <caption>Use alligator-to-breadboard clips to make the electrical
                connections between the leads of the photosensors and your
                breadboard.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/slide12_a6a60c41.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-clips" ref="fig-day16-clips">
    <caption>Use alligator-to-breadboard clips to make the electrical connections between the leads of the photosensors and your breadboard.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 12, then the assembly activity, ≈ 11 min, and the ultimate setup.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-arm">
                <title>Solar tracker assembly</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-cups">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, remove your photocells from the
                    breadboard, put one into each of the two shielding cups on the motor
                    arm, and bring each lead to the breadboard with an
                    alligator-to-breadboard clip, one clip per lead.  If your photocells
                    are already in the cups, they are already wired through their clips;
                    go on to the last step.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-recreate">
                    <statement><p>Connect the leads of the alligator clips to your
                    breadboard to recreate the photocell circuit from earlier in the
                    lab.  Then power up in the usual order and run your two-channel
                    program again: both channels print, now from the arm.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-day16-full-setup">
                <caption>The ultimate setup.  The two photocells are drawn on the
                breadboard, but they are physically in the cups of your tracker arm,
                reached through the alligator clips.  The
                servo is powered from the regulator board's 5V pin and its orange signal
                lead goes to D11 (PA7), as in <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/>.  The
                regulator board is plugged into the breadboard, so each of its pins is
                reached through the row it sits in.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/week8FullLabSetup.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-full-setup" ref="fig-day16-full-setup">
    <caption>The ultimate setup. The two photocells are drawn on the breadboard here, but they are physically in the cups of your tracker arm, reached through the alligator clips; the servo is powered from the regulator board's 5V pin.</caption>
    <note>Her slide 14. Leave up while they recreate the circuit; the checkpoint about minute 70 is the instructor block.</note>
</slide>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-arm-checkpoint">
                <p>Checkpoint about minute 70.  The arm changes no electrical node, so
                every fault here is a clip: on the wrong row, in a rail, or not gripping
                the lead.  A channel at 4095 with its clip in the 3.3 V jumper's row and
                nothing in the node row is the one to look for first; two channels that
                print the same value have their two clips in one row.  A student whose
                servo is not yet working still does this Part; the servo is not needed
                until the loop runs.  Deliverable 8 asks for the maximum and minimum
                voltages, so no table has been asked to observe which channel rises as
                the light moves toward which side; Part 4's discussion reasons the
                sign out instead, from which cell is on A1 and which way the arm turned
                on Day 15 as the knob raised the reading.  Part 4 can open with the
                tables that are finished while the last tables finish their
                clips.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 20 min = 3 (the two loops) + 5 (read Lab 8 §4) + 11 (her slide 15's discussion, three questions) + 1 (the bounds) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-loop">
            <title>Part 4: Read "A Solar Tracker" in the Lab Handout</title>
            <p>
                There are two feedback loops in the tracker
                (<xref ref="fig-tracker-loop-servo"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-tracker-loop-around"/>).  The first loop is inside the
                servo, and it was described in the servo chapter
                (<xref ref="subsec-servo-holds"/>).  The command position goes in, the
                potentiometer on the output shaft measures the position, and the servo's
                own controller drives the motor until the error is zero.  That loop is
                built into the servo.  The second loop is the tracking loop, the one
                that points the arm at the light, and it is Lab 8's Figure 6.  The two
                sensors are read by the two ADC channels, a controller computes a new
                PWM value, the PWM pulse moves the servo, and the angle of the arm sets
                how much light each sensor receives, which closes the loop.  Lab 8's
                Figure 6 calls the two sensors' node voltages <m>V_0</m> and
                <m>V_1</m>, one divider each, on A0 and A1; the error is
                <m>e = V_1 - V_0</m>, and the controller updates the PWM value by
                <m>K\,e</m> every <m>T</m> milliseconds, where <m>T</m> is the sampling
                interval.  The controller in this loop is the program you will write in
                Lab 8.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tracker-loop-servo">
                <caption>The loop inside the servo, from the servo chapter: the command
                position, the error, the controller, the motor, the gears, and the
                potentiometer that feeds the position back.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide08_095020b6.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-loop-servo" ref="fig-tracker-loop-servo" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>There are two feedback loops in the tracker. The first loop is inside the servo, and it was described in the servo chapter. The command position goes in, the potentiometer on the output shaft measures the position, and the servo's own controller drives the motor until the error is zero. That loop is built into the servo.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min.</note>
</slide>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tracker-loop-around">
                <caption>Lab 8's Figure 6, the tracking loop: two sensors, whose node
                voltages are <m>V_0</m> and <m>V_1</m>, two ADC conversions, the
                controller we write, the PWM pulse, the servo, and the angle θ of the
                arm, which feeds back to the sensors through the light.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day16-Photosensors/lab8-fig6-tracker-loop.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-loop-around" ref="fig-tracker-loop-around" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>The second loop is the tracking loop, the one that points the arm at the light, and it is Lab 8's Figure 6. The two sensors are read by the two ADC channels, a controller computes a new PWM value, the PWM pulse moves the servo, and the angle of the arm sets how much light each sensor receives, which closes the loop.</li>
        <li>Lab 8's Figure 6 calls the two sensors' node voltages V_0 and V_1, one divider each, on A0 and A1. The error is e = V_1 − V_0, and the controller updates the PWM value by K·e every T milliseconds, where T is the sampling interval.</li>
        <li>The controller in this loop is the program you will write in Lab 8.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, then her slide 15 as the activity, ≈ 16 min: 5 to read, 11 to discuss.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day16-design">
                <title>Read "A Solar Tracker" in the lab handout</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Read section 4 of Lab 8, <em>Design Challenge – Solar
                    Tracker</em>, and discuss at your table how you might implement the
                    feedback loop.  Three questions to settle in the discussion:</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-sign">
                    <statement><p>Suppose <m>V_1</m> is larger than <m>V_0</m>, so that
                    the error <m>e = V_1 - V_0</m> is positive.  Which way should the
                    arm turn, and should the PWM value increase or decrease to turn it
                    that way?  What do you need to know about your own build to answer
                    that?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-integer">
                    <statement><p>Lab 8 recommends starting with a controller gain <m>K</m> on
                    the order of 0.01, with <m>e</m> in ADC counts and the PWM value in timer counts.
                    With <m>e = 50</m>, what step does <m>K\,e</m> give, in whole
                    timer counts?  For which values of <m>e</m> is the step zero?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day16-tuning">
                    <statement><p>Predict what the arm does if <m>K</m> is much too large,
                    and what it does if the sampling interval <m>T</m> is much too
                    long.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                In Lab 8 you are asked to impose upper and lower bounds on the PWM value
                before it is written to <c>CCR1</c>, TIM14's compare register, so that
                the servo is not driven beyond ±60° of center; your servo program from
                Day 15 already applies a limit in <c>updateServo()</c> before every
                write, and Lab 8's ±60° is narrower than the program's <c>SERVO_MIN</c>
                and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>, so the two numbers are yours to work out from
                Day 15's pulse convention, a 1 ms pulse for −90° and a 2 ms pulse for
                +90° (<xref ref="subsec-servo-ref-command"/>).  You are also
                asked to keep track of <m>T</m> with <c>milliseconds()</c>, the SysTick
                count from the motor chapter (<xref ref="subsec-day12-main-loop"/>).
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-bounds">
    <ul>
        <li>In Lab 8 you are asked to impose upper and lower bounds on the PWM value before it is written to <c>CCR1</c>, TIM14's compare register, so that the servo is not driven beyond ±60° of center. Your servo program from Day 15 already applies a limit in <c>updateServo()</c> before every write, and Lab 8's ±60° is narrower than the program's <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>, so the two numbers are yours to work out from Day 15's pulse convention: a 1 ms pulse is −90° and a 2 ms pulse is +90°.</li>
        <li>You are also asked to keep track of T with <c>milliseconds()</c>, the SysTick count from the motor chapter.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, after the discussion. The three answers are the instructor block.</note>
</slide>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-discussion">
                <p>The three answers, for the discussion's reveal.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><term>The sign.</term>  A positive error means the cell on
                    channel 1 receives more light, so the arm has to turn toward channel
                    1's side.  Whether that is a larger or a smaller PWM value depends on
                    which cell is on A1 and which way the arm turns as the pulse gets
                    wider (the knob, on Day 15), so the sign of K comes from
                    each student's own board.  A wrong sign drives the arm away from the
                    light, once it is off balance, to a bound, where it stays: an arm
                    pinned at one end, not one that hunts.  The fix is to reverse the
                    sign of the step.</li>
                    <li><term>The step.</term>  K e must be a whole number of timer
                    counts, because CCR1 holds an integer.  0.01 × 50 = 0.5 counts,
                    and integer arithmetic gives 0; an <c>int</c> K of 0.01 is 0
                    already.  Written as a division, e/100, the step is zero until |e|
                    reaches 100: a dead zone of about ±100 counts, about 0.08 V
                    (0.8 mV per count), inside which the arm does not correct.
                    Integers are a trade (no floating-point library) with that cost;
                    the Reference has the float alternative.</li>
                    <li><term>K and T.</term>  K too large: each step overshoots, the
                    error changes sign, the arm hunts around the light.  T too long: the
                    arm corrects only every T and lags a moving light.  Lab 8's tuning
                    paragraph says the same and recommends K on the order of 0.01 to
                    start.</li>
                </ul>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day16-loop">
                <p>A worked loop, in the shape Lab 8 describes, with Day 15's
                <c>updateServo()</c> and Lab 5's ADC names.  The sign of the step and
                the two bound values are the student's; <c>K_DIV</c> of 100 is Lab 8's
                K = 0.01 as a division.  <c>pwm</c> and <c>e</c> are signed,
                because <c>e</c> is negative half the time.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define T_MS        100         // sampling interval T, in ms
#define K_DIV       100         // K = 1/K_DIV = 0.01
#define PWM_LOWER   /* their value: 60 degrees below center */
#define PWM_UPPER   /* their value: 60 degrees above center */

    int32_t  v0, v1, e;
    int32_t  pwm = SERVO_MID;
    uint32_t last_update = milliseconds();

    while (1) {
        if (milliseconds() - last_update >= T_MS) {
            last_update = milliseconds();
            adc_setChannel(0);
            v0 = adc_getValue();
            adc_setChannel(1);
            v1 = adc_getValue();
            e = v1 - v0;
            pwm = pwm + e / K_DIV;              // or pwm - e / K_DIV: the sign is found on the board
            if (pwm > PWM_UPPER) pwm = PWM_UPPER;
            if (pwm < PWM_LOWER) pwm = PWM_LOWER;
            updateServo((uint16_t)pwm);
        }
    }
]]></code></program>
                <p>The fixed-step version replaces the update with
                <c>if (e &gt; 0) pwm = pwm + STEP; else if (e &lt; 0) pwm = pwm - STEP;</c>,
                which has no dead zone and, because <c>e</c> is almost never exactly
                zero, dithers by one step about the balance point.  What to expect on
                the demo: with K_DIV of 100 the arm settles inside a band of about 100
                counts of error and stops; halving K_DIV halves the band and, past some
                value, starts the hunting.  A competing light, Lab 8's warning, shows up
                as an arm that settles pointing between the flashlight and a window or a
                laptop screen.  A student whose servo still does not run pairs with a
                tablemate's board for the sign observation and predicts the sign from
                the pulse convention alone.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5: 15 min of lab work time, then the close (5 min) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day16-start">
            <title>Part 5: Starting on Your Tracker</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll start on the loop, which is your lab work in Lab 8.  In
                Lab 8, Deliverable 9 is a demonstration of the working tracker,
                with a log of your tuning of <m>K</m> and <m>T</m>, and Deliverable 10
                puts the seven-segment display back into the system to show the servo's
                angle.  When you test your system make sure that your flashlight is
                brighter than any competing light source; a laptop screen, a lamp across
                the room and a sunny window may all attract the tracker.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day16-start">
    <ul>
        <li>Now we'll start on the loop, which is your lab work in Lab 8.</li>
        <li>In Lab 8, Deliverable 9 is a demonstration of the working tracker, with a log of your tuning of K and T, and Deliverable 10 puts the seven-segment display back into the system to show the servo's angle.</li>
        <li>When you test your system make sure that your flashlight is brighter than any competing light source; a laptop screen, a lamp across the room and a sunny window may all attract the tracker.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then ≈ 14 min of lab time; leave up. Lab 8 is next; no other homework is due (Petra, 2026-09-03).</note>
</slide>
